\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{The Diminishing Weakest Link.}
At $K{=}3$, a single compromised judge flips the majority ($t{=}2$), making $\eta_{\max}$ a strong predictor. At $K{\geq}5$, no single judge is pivotal; $\eta_{\max}$'s pooled effect declines monotonically ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$: $0.264 \to 0.102 \to 0.084$; CI crosses zero at $K{\geq}5$). Diversity ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) is positively associated with vulnerability in all 12 conditions (OLS and GAM bootstrap 12/12), but significance is method-sensitive (0--12/12; bootstrap 4/12 at $K{=}3$, 7/12 at $K{=}5$). No magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$; Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_keff_eta_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Panel-level correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ at $K{=}5$ ($n{=}3{,}003$ panels). Full model pool (gray, $r{=}0.81$) exhibits strong collinearity; capability-matched Pool~A (orange, $r{=}0.50$, 126 panels) shows reduced confounding.}
\label{fig:keff_eta_scatter}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Diversity--Capability Confound: A Fundamental Limitation.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ correlate at $r{=}0.70$--$0.81$ (variance inflation factor, VIF${\approx}2.0$--$2.9$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}). Cinelli--Hazlett sensitivity analysis \citep{cinelli2020making} shows the confound could account for the association in 69\% of conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}), though fractional logit confirms the positive association (9/12 at all $K$). Pool~A ($r{=}0.50$) raises the robustness value (RV) in 10/12 at $K{=}5$ despite max 30.1\% power (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_poolA}); cross-benchmark instrumental variable (IV) analysis provides additional identification (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_benchmark}), though causal attribution requires capability-controlled designs. The structural mechanism---maximizing diversity forces sampling across capability tiers, systematically introducing weaker judges---is detailed in Appendix~\ref{app:diversity_capability}.

\paragraph{API-Only vs.\ Full Pool.}
Restricting to 9 API models removes the AWQ confound but increases collinearity ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$): $\eta_{\max}$ retains significance (8/12) while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ drops (2--3/12; Table~\ref{tab:robustness_checks}). A quantization dummy attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ ($+0.39 \to +0.34 \to +0.27$; Appendix~\ref{app:quant_dummy}), confirming partial confounding, though extreme collinearity (VIF${=}162$ at $K{=}7$) limits interpretation. The API-only pool lacks power (MDE $> 0.897$ at 80\%; Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_panel_design.pdf}
\caption{Panel ASR under three selection strategies. $\min(\eta_{\max})$ achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions, confirming robustness-first selection dominates diversity-maximizing strategies.}
\label{fig:panel_design}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
Aggregation rules influence predictability but not $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (Figure~\ref{fig:aggregation}); at $K{=}7$ Bayesian calibration neutralizes it (0/12), though the posterior concentration ratio declines only modestly ($0.890 \to 0.867$; Appendix~\ref{app:bayesian_entropy}). Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in all conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:panel_design}; full strategy comparison in Appendix~\ref{app:panel_selection}); clean $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is stable under 4/6 attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}), and $\eta_{\max}$ retains predictive power even under partial panel information (Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).

\paragraph{Metric Sensitivity of the Diversity Trajectory.}
The diversity significance trajectory is operationalization-dependent: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ increases ($4 \to 7 \to 8$/12), $\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} decreases ($8 \to 7 \to 6$/12; Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_regime}), Shannon MI entropy shows a third pattern ($4 \to 4 \to 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:shannon_entropy}). A pooled comparison of all alternative measures appears in Appendix~\ref{app:alt_diversity}. Both diversity metrics confirm positive direction (12/12), but the $K$-dependent trajectory is metric-specific. The $\eta_{\max}$ decline holds under both specifications, making it the more robust finding. Excluding near-chance quantized models (MMLU $< 0.35$) strengthens $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient at $K \leq 5$ (Appendix~\ref{app:near_chance}), confirming that the association is not an artifact of non-functional judges.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Weakest-Link Dominance at \texorpdfstring{$K{=}3$}{K=3}}
\label{sec:dominance}

Table~\ref{tab:dominance} reports the log-log OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) for all 12 conditions across the 455 panels ($K{=}3$, 15 judges from Table~\ref{tab:models}).

Under OLS, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is positive and FDR-significant in all 12/12 conditions. $\eta_{\max}$ is significant in 10/12 (exceptions: MMLU $\times$ score manipulation and ARC $\times$ position bias), consistent with Proposition~\ref{thm:monotonicity}. Dominance analysis yields 10 co-dominant, 2 $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant, and zero $\eta_{\max}$-dominant conditions. However, the $r = 0.70$ $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ correlation raises confound concerns, and OLS ignores the non-independence of the 455 panels (Appendix~\ref{app:keff_dist}).

Each judge appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ of the 455 panels, and each pair co-occurs in 13. This shared structure causes OLS to underestimate standard errors by approximately $2.5{\times}$. Figure~\ref{fig:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections: both predictors attenuate under bootstrap ($\eta_{\max}$ 3/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 4/12), with clustered SE widest ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 0/12; Appendix~\ref{app:forest_plot}). A crossed random-effects analysis accounting for the multi-membership structure of shared judges confirms $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance at $K{\geq}5$ under this more conservative correction (Appendix~\ref{app:crossed_re}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} \\
\midrule
OLS                    & 10/12 & 12/12 \\
Clustered SE           & 4/12  & 0/12  \\
Mixed effects          & 7/12  & 3/12  \\
Wild bootstrap         & 3/12  & 4/12  \\
Frac.\ logit (clust.)  & 7/12  & 9/12  \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Inference method sensitivity at $K{=}3$ (455 panels, 12 conditions). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance ranges from 0/12 (clustered SE) to 12/12 (OLS); all methods preserve positive direction (12/12).}
\label{tab:method_comparison}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_robustness_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Significance counts (FDR $< 0.05$) for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across four correction methods over 12 experimental conditions ($K{=}3$). Wild cluster bootstrap and clustered standard errors are recommended for the 15-judge combinatorial design.}
\label{fig:robustness}
\end{figure}

Power analysis yields a minimum detectable effect (MDE) of $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power (decreasing to 0.048 at $K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}). The bootstrap attenuation at $K{=}3$ (OLS 12/12 $\to$ bootstrap 4/12) is consistent with this MDE, though positive coefficient direction (12/12) is preserved across all methods (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}). Monte Carlo calibration (1{,}000 permutations per condition) confirms the wild cluster bootstrap is conservative: the maximum null rejection rate is $1.6\%$ at nominal $\alpha{=}5\%$ across all 36 conditions ($12 \times 3$ panel sizes), so reported significance counts are lower bounds (Appendix~\ref{app:monte_carlo}).

\subsection{The Diminishing Weakest Link Across Panel Sizes}
\label{sec:cross_k}

Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison} reveals that the $K{=}3$ pattern is method-sensitive: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance ranges from 0/12 (clustered SE) to 12/12 (OLS), with fractional logit giving 9/12. This contrasts with $K{\geq}5$, where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s dominance is consistent across methods (bootstrap 7/12 at $K{=}5$; fractional logit 9/12).

We repeat the wild cluster bootstrap at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, constructing all $\binom{15}{K}$ panels with $K$-position max-$p$ correction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_regime_transition.pdf}
\caption{Predictor shift across panel sizes. $\eta_{\max}$ significance (orange) declines from 3/12 to 2/12, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (blue) rises from 4/12 to 7/12 as $K$ increases from 3 to 5. Shaded region marks the predictor shift zone $K^* \in (3, 5)$. Bootstrap: $B{=}9999$, Rademacher weights, position-permutation max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$.}
\label{fig:regime_transition}
\end{figure}

Meta-analytic pooling (DerSimonian--Laird random effects across 12 conditions) quantifies the trajectories independent of significance thresholds. $\eta_{\max}$'s pooled $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ declines monotonically: $0.264$ [$0.158, 0.369$] at $K{=}3$ ($I^2{=}91.3\%$), $0.102$ [$-0.055, 0.259$] at $K{=}5$ ($I^2{=}99.4\%$), $0.084$ [$-0.083, 0.251$] at $K{=}7$ ($I^2{=}99.8\%$); CI crosses zero at $K{\geq}5$. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s pooled coefficient remains positive: $0.356$ [$0.267, 0.445$] ($I^2{=}87.7\%$), $0.436$ [$0.318, 0.553$] ($I^2{=}98.9\%$), $0.401$ [$0.298, 0.503$] ($I^2{=}99.4\%$)---heterogeneous magnitudes, consistent direction. The high $I^2$ values ($87.7$--$99.8\%$) indicate substantial between-condition heterogeneity; pooled estimates summarize the central trend but individual conditions vary widely, so they should be interpreted as directional evidence rather than precise universal effect sizes. Figure~\ref{fig:regime_transition} shows significance counts (per-condition breakdown in Figure~\ref{fig:dominance_heatmap}, Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}). The $\eta_{\max}$ decline holds under both $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity specifications, providing a power-independent characterization. Restricting to the four $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-stable attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}) confirms the signal does not depend on measurement-unstable conditions.

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

We simulate non-adversarial noise by randomly flipping judge votes at observed per-judge error rates. Random faults yield $R^2 = 0.730$ [$0.718, 0.745$] ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant 2/2); adaptive attack reduces predictability ($R^2{=}0.285$ [$0.270, 0.301$] at $K{=}5$) but $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains power (7/12). The predictability gap is $\Delta{=}0.446$ [$0.430, 0.460$]. The positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient under adaptive attack contrasts with the Condorcet Jury Theorem \citep{condorcet1785essai}, where independence among voters is protective. Under adversarial manipulation, independent judges present distinct attack surfaces rather than redundant defenses; this reversal is consistent with adversarial ensemble theory \citep{goodfellow2015adversarial,kariyappa2019improving}.

\subsection{Positive Direction Across Attack Types}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM bootstrap confirms positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction in all 12 conditions (88.9--100\% positive rate; Figure~\ref{fig:gam_split}), with no magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$, $0.423$).

\subsection{Aggregation Rules and the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 11+/0$-$  & 0.829 \\
Majority        & 10/12 & 12/12 & 12+/0$-$  & 0.327 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 12/12 & 12+/0$-$  & 0.328 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 6/12  & 5+/1$-$   & 0.087 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison ($K{=}3$, 15 models, 455 panels, OLS log-log). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Table~\ref{tab:dominance} uses per-condition FDR; this table uses pooled FDR across conditions.}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}


Predictability ranges from threshold ($R^2{=}0.829$) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.087$). The Bayesian $R^2$ near zero implies that panel composition is almost unpredictive under calibrated aggregation---the diversity and weakest-link predictors may only be relevant under specific aggregation rules rather than being intrinsic panel properties. Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), yielding partial defense (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}). At $K{=}5$, all four methods converge to identical $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (7/12; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k5}); at $K{=}7$, methods diverge again, with Bayesian neutralization returning while threshold voting strengthens (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k7}).

\paragraph{Fractional Logit Robustness.}
Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE shows consistent positive association: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance is 9/12 across all panel sizes ($K{=}3,5,7$), with 98.6\% directional concordance with OLS (71/72; Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}).

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge and Multi-Judge Evaluation.}
LLM judges gained traction after \citet{zheng2023judging} established them as scalable alternatives to human annotation. \citet{wang2023fair} documented systematic failure modes---position bias, verbosity, sycophancy---motivating multi-judge panels, while \citet{raina2024judgerobust} and \citet{maloyan2025investigating} quantified adversarial vulnerabilities including prompt injection. Multi-judge aggregation extends the classical noisy annotator framework \citep{dawid1979maximum}: \citet{verga2024replacing} showed diverse panels can match single large judges, and \citet{panickssery2024llm} identified self-preference bias driving within-family correlations. Most relevant, \citet{almasoud2026security} reported that 7-model committees reduce ASR from 50--68\% to 10--19\% but did not disentangle diversity from individual robustness---the gap we address. Concurrent work benchmarks judge quality \citep{tan2024judgebench,li2024autoj}, adversarial risks in multi-agent systems \citep{tamas2025benchmarking}, and collaborative evaluation under benign conditions \citep{qian2026collabeval}; none tests whether aggregation benefits survive adversarial manipulation.

\paragraph{Condorcet Jury Theorem.}
\citet{condorcet1785essai} proved that majority voting accuracy converges to 1 when per-voter accuracy exceeds 0.5; \citet{ladha1992condorcet} extended the theorem to correlated voters, showing that positive correlation weakens the convergence guarantee. The Condorcet Jury Theorem (CJT) explicitly assumes independent, non-adversarial errors; its failure under adaptive adversaries is trivially expected \citep{tramer2018ensemble,kariyappa2019improving,pang2019improving}. We focus on the \emph{panel-size-dependent structure} of this vulnerability: the diminishing predictive power of the weakest link as $K$ grows, and the persistent positive association between diversity and vulnerability.

\paragraph{Ensemble Diversity Theory.}
Classical ensemble theory shows diverse ensembles reduce error \citep{dietterich2000ensemble,breiman2001random,krogh1995neural}, but only when members maintain sufficiently low bias \citep{brown2005managing}; selective ensembles can outperform using all members \citep{zhou2002ensembling}. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} found no pairwise diversity measure reliably predicted improvement; our $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a $K$-dependent pattern whose direction depends on the metric. LLM panels differ from classical ensembles: members are heterogeneous pre-trained models, and adversaries exploit natural-language manipulation rather than random noise. Recent frameworks \citep{yang2026diversity,turkmen2026ensemble} characterize diversity under benign evaluation; we provide the adversarial complement.

\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness and Transferability.}
Adversarial vulnerability was formalized by \citet{goodfellow2015adversarial}, with transferability demonstrated by \citet{papernot2016transferability,papernot2017practical}. Controlled diversity via uncorrelated gradients \citep{kariyappa2019improving}, diversity regularization \citep{pang2019improving}, or vulnerability diversification \citep{zhu2020dverge} strengthens ensemble robustness in vision but requires coordinating member training---a control absent in post-hoc LLM judge selection. Our setting differs: adversaries use natural-language manipulation rather than gradient perturbations, and aggregation rules are a controllable design parameter.

\paragraph{BFT and Security Games.}
Classical BFT \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical,blanchard2017byzantine} tolerates faulty nodes but assumes non-strategic faults; \citet{schneier2000secrets} articulated the weakest-link principle. Our framework adopts the Stackelberg structure of \citet{tambe2011security}. Correlated LLM judge errors \citep{kim2025correlated,kuai2026independent} violate BFT's independence assumption; \citet{kuai2026independent} propose the Difficulty-Weighted Behavioral Entanglement Index (DBEI), weighting agreement by item difficulty, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ captures a complementary dimension via pairwise MI. Mitigation strategies include DBEI-based reweighting, Bayesian calibration, and credibility scoring \citep{ebrahimi2025adversary}.

\paragraph{Information-Theoretic Evaluation.}
Information-theoretic approaches address statistical validity \citep{robertson2025stepwise,feng2026noisy}, attack costs \citep{kaneko2025bits}, sequential selection \citep{jung2026logarithmic}, and truthfulness \citep{chen2025incentivizing,qiu2026truthfulness}, but assume honest judges. We address adversarial manipulation interacting with multi-judge composition.

